{"text": "\u2018Am, J. Hum, Genet, \u00a72:24-38, 1993,\n\nIdentification of the Transcriptional Unit, Structural\nOrganization, and Promoter Sequence of the Human\nSex-determining Region Y (SRY) Gene, Using a Reverse\nGenetic Approach\n\nHua Su and Yun-Fai Chris Lau\n\nDiviion of Cel nd Developmencal Geneces, Department of Meine, VA Medal Cencer, Universiy of Calforn, San Francico\n\n\u2018Summary\n\nUsing a simple strategy involving cosmid-mediated gene transfer, cDNA library construction, and molecular\ncharacterization techniques, we have determined the transcriptional unit, structural organization, and\npromoter sequence of the human sex-determining region Y (SRY) gene, the putative testis-determining factor\n(TDF) gene on the human Y chromosome. By this approach, a recombinant cosmid harboring the human\nSRY sequence was isolated and transfected to appropriate tissue-cultured cells. Recombinant cDNA clones\n\u2018were isolated from a eDNA library constructed from poly (A) + RNA of the transfected cells. Comparative\nstudies between the respective cDNAs and the genomic cosmid have provided information regarding the\n\u201corganization of the SRY gene and its mRNAs. The results indicate that the human SRY gene is an intronless\n\u2018gene, produces transcripts of 1.1 kb, and possesses promoter activitis in the transfected cells at approximately\n\n310 bp of its upstream sequences.\n\nIntroduction\n\nThe sex-determining region \u00a5 (SRY) gene wasisolated\nfrom the sex-determining region of the human Y chro-\nmosome by analysis of 35 kb of DNA adjacent to\nthe pseudoautosomal boundary of this chromosome\n(Sinclair etal. 1990). Previous studies using deletion\n\u2018mapping and chromosome walking had identified this\nregion t0 harbor the testis-determining factor (TDF)\n\u2018gene, the primary switch gene responsible for initiat-\ning testis determination and differentiation in. man\n(Palmer et al. 1989), Random sequencing of unique\nsequences within this portion of the human genome\nidentified an evolutionary conserved 2.1-kb DNA\nfragment, pY53.3, which contains two open reading\n\nReceived June 11, 1992; fl evsion recived September 23,\n1992,\nAddress for corespondence and reprints: De. Chis Lau, Divi-\nson of Cel and Detelopmental Genes, VA Medical Center,\nIIIGS, University of California, San Francisco, 4150 Clement\nSecee, San Francisco, CA 94121\n{\u00a9'199) by The Ameci Sxty of Haman Genesis, Al ih rere\n(002-9297/93/5201- 000450200.\n\n4\n\nframes (ORFs) for 99 and 223 amino acids in its se\n\u00abquence (Sinclair etal. 1990). The larger ORF encodes\na protein containing a sequence of 120 amino acids\nthatis homologoustoaDNA-binding motif\u2014the high\nmobility group (HMG) box\u2014present in several non-\nhistone chromosomal proteins and transcription fac-\ntors (Johns 1982; Tsuda et al. 1988; van Holde 1989;\nJantzen et al. 1990} Bachvarov and Moss 1991; Parisi\nland Clayton 1991; Travisecal. 1991; vande Wetering,\netal. 1991). A pocential splice acceptor site wasidenti-\nfied at the 5\u201d end of this ORF. Hence, the sequence\npresent in the pY53.3 fragment has been postulated\n10 be the last exon of the SRY gene.\nEvidence for the SRY gene to be the TDF gene has\nbeen derived from studies of the homologous gene,\nSy, in the mouse. Genetic mapping studies demon-\nstrated that the mouse Sry gene is deleted in a special\nline of XY sex-reversed female mice and is present\nin another sex-reversed strain of XXsxr\u2019 male mice\n(Gubbay et al. 1990). The Sry gene is expressed in the\nmale gonadal ridge at the time of sex determination\nin fetal mice (Koopman et al. 1990). Several partial\n\u2018DNAs for the Sry and related geneshave been isolated",
